getName": "Sīra Literature", "context": "Hassan ibn Thabit's poems transmitted crucial details and perspectives that were later incorporated into sīra (Prophet's biography) literature by historians like Ibn Ishaq."}, {"sourceSlug": "quraysh-tribe", "sourceName": "Quraysh Tribe", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "hassan-ibn-thabit", "targetName": "Hassan ibn Thabit", "context": "The opposition and lampoons from the Quraysh tribe directly prompted Hassan ibn Thabit to compose retaliatory and defensive poetry for the early Muslim community."}], "places": [{"name": "Medina, Arabia", "role": "Center of poetic activity"}, {"name": "Mecca, Arabia", "role": "Subject of poetic lampoons"}, {"name": "Arabian Peninsula", "role": "Geographic scope of influence"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Hassan ibn Thabit", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "hassan-ibn-thabit"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q2315457", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hassan_ibn_Thabit"], "tags": ["Poets & Playwrights", "textual-transmission", "cultural-diffusion", "temporal-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Hassan ibn Thabit's poetry served as a crucial ideological weapon and historical record during the formative years of Islam, influencing the narrative of the Prophet's life and shaping subsequent Arabic poetic traditions. His works remain a primary source for understanding 7th-century Arabian society and the early Muslim community.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-11-22T22:10:45Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#45076022738", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Hassan ibn Thabit was an Arabian poet and one of the companions of the Islamic prophet Muhammad, who was best known for poems in defense of the prophet.", "newValue": "Hassan ibn Thabit (died 674 CE), an esteemed Arabian poet, emerged as a pivotal figure in the nascent Islamic community, earning the title 'Poet of the Prophet' (Sha'ir al-Rasul). Born into the Banu Khazraj tribe in Yathrib (later Medina), he initially gained renown in pre-Islamic Arabia for his eloquent verses, but his significance was irrevocably shaped by his conversion to Islam. He became the primary poetic voice defending the Islamic prophet Muhammad and the fledgling Muslim community against their detractors, particularly the Quraysh polytheists, during the tumultuous period of the early 7th century CE.

His poetic contributions were not merely aesthetic; they served as a crucial form of ideological and psychological warfare. Hassan ibn Thabit composed numerous panegyrics (madīḥ) celebrating Muhammad's virtues and the triumphs of Islam, while simultaneously crafting scathing lampoons (hijāʾ) against the Prophet's enemies. His verses chronicled key events of the sīra (Prophet's biography), including the Hijra, the Battle of the Trench (627 CE), and the Conquest of Mecca (630 CE), offering vivid, contemporary accounts that resonated deeply with the early Muslim faithful and demoralized opponents. Muhammad himself endorsed Hassan's poetic efforts, reportedly saying, 'Recite, O Hassan, for the Holy Spirit is with you.'

